{# ============================================================================
   PEBBLE PRODUCT TABS · CAROUSEL — one rail per tab, of real product cards
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Hot This Week                            ( Best Sellers ) New Arrivals
     ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌──
     │ [New]  │ │        │ │ [Sale] │ │ [Hot]  │ │
     │ [Popul]│ │   🔍   │ │        │ │        │ │   ← the 5th is cut by the
     │        │ │        │ │        │ │        │ │     viewport: that sliver
     │        │ │(Add ▸+)│ │        │ │        │ │     IS the scroll hint
     └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘ └──
      Logo Polo   Stripe Hat  Backpack   Backpack
      $45.00      $15.00      $22 $32    $55.00
      ▬           ▬           ▬          ▬
     ▬▬▬───────────────                        ( ‹ ) ( › )

   Measured off the reference at 1440px:
     container  1200, and the rail runs from its start edge to the viewport's
     cards      four across it with a 10px gap — which is where 292.5px comes
                from, not the other way round
     tile       3:4, rounded 12
     tabs       32px pills on the head's end edge, the active one on #E7E7E7
     progress   225px, 2px tall, at the start edge
     arrows     38px circles at the end edge, on the progress bar's own row

   ── It shares its rail with the collection tabs ──
   The shell (`.hy-ct-*`, assets/style.css → 15.10) and the behaviour
   (`HayatRail`, assets/main.js) are the same two sections' worth of code
   serving both, because they are the same thing over different cards. This one
   adds `.hy-ct--products`, which is the measured difference: four across
   instead of five, a 10px gap, a 225px indicator and 38px arrows.

   ── Tabs ──
   Three fixed slots (`tab1_*` … `tab3_*`), NOT repeatable blocks.

   ⚠️  They were blocks, each holding its own `products` picker, and the Theme
   Editor would not render the block's settings at all with one there. No
   original widget in this theme puts `products` inside a `blocks` entry —
   `media` and `menu` are the only pickers that appear in one — so the type is
   supported at widget level only. Do not move these back into blocks.

   A slot with no LABEL is not a tab and renders nothing. With fewer than two
   labelled slots no tab strip renders at all and the section is just a rail —
   which is what pages/product.json wants for "قد يعجبك أيضاً".

   Every set is rendered SERVER-SIDE and the inactive rails are `hidden`, not
   fetched on click: the sets are already in the page JSON, so a request would
   be a round trip to redisplay what the browser is holding.

   ── Cards ──
   ui/product-card.njk on the server, HayatCard.build() over AJAX. Both live in
   one place each; this widget never writes card markup itself. `catalog` is
   the variant — see 15.9.1.

   ── Empty states ──
   A merchant drops this in before picking anything, so an empty tab falls back
   to fetching the newest products. If the STORE is empty too, the rail draws
   placeholder cards rather than a bare apology: the merchant sees the real
   layout at the real size, waiting to be filled.

   All styling lives in assets/style.css → "15.10 COLLECTION TABS" (the shell)
   and "15.9.1 CATALOG VARIANT" (the cards).
   ========================================================================= #}

{% set id = widget.id %}
{% set d = widget.data %}

{# Fixed slots, listed once here so every loop below stays in step. #}
{% set tabs = [
  { label: d.tab1_label | default(''), products: d.tab1_products | default([]) },
  { label: d.tab2_label | default(''), products: d.tab2_products | default([]) },
  { label: d.tab3_label | default(''), products: d.tab3_products | default([]) }
] %}
{# Counted with an inline-if sum rather than a loop: Nunjucks scopes `{% set %}`
   to the block it runs in, so a counter incremented inside `{% for %}` reads
   back as its pre-loop value outside it. #}
{% set labelled = (1 if d.tab1_label else 0) + (1 if d.tab2_label else 0) + (1 if d.tab3_label else 0) %}
{% set showTabs = labelled > 1 %}
{# Which slot opens. NOT `loop.first` — a merchant who labels slots 2 and 3 and
   leaves slot 1 blank renders no rail for slot 1, and `loop.first` then marks
   nothing, so every rail kept its `hidden` and the section rendered as a bare
   heading. A plain expression, because a loop that found it could not report
   back out: Nunjucks scopes `{% set %}` to the block it runs in. #}
{% set firstIdx = 0 if d.tab1_label else (1 if d.tab2_label else (2 if d.tab3_label else 0)) %}

{% set title = d.title | default('') %}
{% set subtitle = d.subtitle | default('') %}
{% set viewAllText = d.view_all_text | default('') %}
{% set viewAllUrl = d.view_all_url | default('/shop') %}

{% set per = d.columns_desktop | default('4') | float %}
{% set perM = d.columns_mobile | default('2') | float %}
{% set ratio = d.card_image_ratio | default('3x4') %}
{% set bg = d.background | default('white') %}
{% set showArrows = d.show_arrows | default(true) %}
{% set showBar = d.show_progress | default(true) %}

{# ---- Fallback size ----
   Only ever applies to the automatic "newest products" fallback; a hand-picked
   list is shown in full. It is both SENT to the API and applied with `slice` —
   see the note beside the request. #}
{% set limit = 12 %}

{% set promoOn = d.promo_enabled | default(false) %}
{% set promoImg = d.promo_image.fileUrl if d.promo_image and d.promo_image.fileUrl else (d.promo_image if d.promo_image is string else '') %}
{# Demo picture while the field is empty: assets/demo-*.jpg are CC0 photos
   (sources in _ref/demo-images-sources.txt). An uploaded image replaces it. #}
{# demo-promo shows a girl (house rule: no women or girls), so it stays unused. #}
{% set promoImg = promoImg if promoImg else ('demo-bundle.jpg' | assets) %}
{% set promoUrl = d.promo_url | default('/shop') %}


<section id="hy-pt-{{ id }}"
         class="hy-ct hy-ct--products {{ 'hy-ct--well' if bg == 'well' }}"
         data-hy-rail-root>
  <style>
    /* NOTE: The `-set` suffix is load-bearing. This is an ID selector, so anything
       it writes outranks every class rule in the stylesheet — the responsive
       ones included. Written as `--hy-ct-per` directly, a desktop count of 4
       survived into a 390px viewport and put four cards on a phone.

       The stylesheet reads these as the value for EACH band and decides
       nothing else; the fraction on the phone count is what leaves a sliver of
       the next card showing, which is how a rail says it scrolls on a screen
       with no scrollbar and no hover. */
    #hy-pt-{{ id }} {
      --hy-ct-per-set: {{ per }};
      --hy-ct-per-md-set: {{ 3 if per > 3 else per }};
      --hy-ct-per-sm-set: {{ perM + 0.2 }};
    }
  </style>

  {# ---- head ---- #}
  {% if title or subtitle or showTabs or viewAllText %}
    <div class="hy-container">
      <div class="hy-ct-head">
        <div class="hy-pt-headings">
          {% if title %}<h2 class="hy-ct-title">{{ title }}</h2>{% endif %}
          {% if subtitle %}<p class="hy-pt-sub">{{ subtitle }}</p>{% endif %}
        </div>

        {# The tab strip and the "view all" link share the head's end edge, so
           only one of them can have it. Tabs win when there are any: they
           change what the rail SHOWS, while the link only leaves it. #}
        {% if showTabs %}
          <div class="hy-ct-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="{{ title | default(t('products.shop'), true) }}">
            {# Unlabelled slots are skipped, but `loop.index0` still counts them
               so the tab indices stay in step with the rails below. There is no
               fallback label on purpose — a shared one makes every unnamed tab
               read identically. #}
            {% for tb in tabs %}
              {% if tb.label %}
                <button type="button"
                        class="hy-ct-tab {{ 'is-active' if loop.index0 == firstIdx }}"
                        role="tab"
                        id="hy-ptTab-{{ id }}-{{ loop.index0 }}"
                        data-hy-tab="{{ loop.index0 }}"
                        aria-controls="hy-ptRail-{{ id }}-{{ loop.index0 }}"
                        aria-selected="{{ 'true' if loop.index0 == firstIdx else 'false' }}">{{ tb.label }}</button>
              {% endif %}
            {% endfor %}
          </div>
        {% elif viewAllText %}
          <a href="{{ viewAllUrl }}" class="hy-ct-all">
            <span>{{ viewAllText }}</span>
            <svg class="rtl-flip" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                 stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="m9 6 6 6-6 6"/>
            </svg>
          </a>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </div>
  {% endif %}

  {# ---- rails: one per tab ----
     Slot 1 renders even unlabelled, so a section with no tabs at all is still
     a product rail. #}
  <div class="hy-ct-rails">
    {% for tb in tabs %}
      {% if tb.label or (loop.first and labelled == 0) %}
        {% set products = tb.products %}
        {% set hasServer = (products | length) > 0 %}
        {% set outer = loop %}

        <div class="hy-ct-rail taqa-scroll-thin"
             id="hy-ptRail-{{ id }}-{{ loop.index0 }}"
             data-hy-rail="{{ loop.index0 }}"
             data-hy-pane="{{ id }}-{{ loop.index0 }}"
             role="{{ 'tabpanel' if showTabs else 'group' }}"
             {% if showTabs %}aria-labelledby="hy-ptTab-{{ id }}-{{ loop.index0 }}"{% endif %}
             {% if loop.index0 != firstIdx %}hidden{% endif %}>

          {% if promoOn and loop.index0 == firstIdx and promoImg %}
            {# The promo tile rides in the FIRST slot of the rail rather than
               sitting outside it: outside it would need its own column and the
               rail's scroll would start past it, which on a phone hides it
               entirely behind the first product. #}
            <a href="{{ promoUrl }}" class="hy-promo">
              <img src="{{ promoImg }}" alt="{{ d.promo_title | default('') }}" loading="lazy" class="hy-promo-img"/>
              <span class="hy-promo-scrim"></span>
              <span class="hy-promo-body">
                {% if d.promo_eyebrow %}<span class="hy-promo-eyebrow">{{ d.promo_eyebrow }}</span>{% endif %}
                {% if d.promo_title %}<span class="hy-promo-title">{{ d.promo_title }}</span>{% endif %}
              </span>
              {% if d.promo_cta_text %}
                <span class="hy-promo-cta">{{ d.promo_cta_text }}</span>
              {% endif %}
            </a>
          {% endif %}

          {% for p in products %}
            {# `show_eyebrow='off'` — the reference puts nothing between the
               photograph and the name, and a collection line there competes
               with the tab the shopper just chose. #}
            {% ui "product-card.njk", product=p, image_ratio=ratio, variant='catalog', show_eyebrow='off' %}
          {% endfor %}

          {% if not hasServer %}
            {# Placeholders for a store with nothing in it yet. They live INSIDE
               the rail so it has its true height from the first paint; the
               script replaces them the moment real products arrive, and leaves
               them standing if none ever do. #}
            {% ui "placeholder.njk", kind='product', count=per, image_ratio=ratio, variant='catalog' %}
          {% endif %}
        </div>
      {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  {# ---- foot ---- #}
  {% if showBar or showArrows %}
    <div class="hy-container">
      <div class="hy-ct-foot">
        {% if showBar %}
          <span class="hy-ct-track" role="presentation">
            <span class="hy-ct-bar" data-hy-bar></span>
          </span>
        {% endif %}

        {% if showArrows %}
          <span class="hy-ct-arrows">
            <button type="button" class="hy-ct-arrow" data-hy-prev aria-label="{{ t('common.prev') }}">
              <svg class="rtl-flip" width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" aria-hidden="true">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15 6 9 12l6 6"/>
              </svg>
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="hy-ct-arrow" data-hy-next aria-label="{{ t('common.next') }}">
              <svg class="rtl-flip" width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" aria-hidden="true">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="m9 6 6 6-6 6"/>
              </svg>
            </button>
          </span>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</section>

{# The rail's behaviour — the tabs, the arrows and the progress bar — is
   HayatRail in assets/main.js, which this section opts into with the
   `data-hy-rail-*` attributes above. Only the fallback fetch is local, because
   only this section has one. #}
<script>
  (function () {
    var root = document.getElementById('hy-pt-{{ id }}');
    if (!root) return;

    /* Mirrors the locals passed to ui "product-card.njk" above, so an AJAX card
       and a server card resolve their options identically. An empty ratio means
       "inherit the theme setting", which HayatCard already does through its own
       defaults — so it must not be sent as an empty string. */
    var cardOpts = { variant: 'catalog', showEyebrow: false };
    {% if ratio %}cardOpts.imageRatio = '{{ ratio }}';{% endif %}

    var LIMIT = {{ limit | int }};

    /* Only the rails that rendered with nothing picked. */
    var empty = [];
    root.querySelectorAll('[data-hy-rail]').forEach(function (rail) {
      if (!rail.querySelector('[data-hy-placeholder]')) return;
      empty.push(rail);
    });
    if (!empty.length) return;

    /* HayatAuto (main.js) makes the one product request the page shares, and
       hands each empty rail the NEXT run of it, so two tabs left empty do not
       show the same cards. It also knows not to send `limit` — the search
       endpoint rejects it, which is why this fallback used to fill nothing.
       Deferred to DOMContentLoaded: main.js loads after this script. */
    function fill() {
      if (!window.HayatAuto) return;
      empty.forEach(function (rail) {
        HayatAuto.take(LIMIT).then(function (list) {
          if (!list.length) return;              /* leave the placeholders standing */
          /* Keep the promo tile — it rides in the rail's first slot. */
          var promo = rail.querySelector('.hy-promo');
          rail.innerHTML = list.map(function (p) {
            return HayatCard.build(p, cardOpts);
          }).join('');
          if (promo) rail.insertBefore(promo, rail.firstChild);
          /* The bar and the arrows were sized against placeholders. Re-running
             the controller is free — it flags the root, so this only
             re-measures. */
          window.dispatchEvent(new Event('resize'));
        });
      });
    }
    if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
      document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', fill);
    } else {
      fill();
    }
  })();
</script>

{# Per-section palette. Emits nothing unless `colors_enabled` is on, so the
   switch IS the revert. Must stay after the widget's own <style>. #}
{% ui "section-colors.njk", scope='#hy-pt-' ~ id, c=d %}
